      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786684629795,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sources/Sinks",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Sources/Sinks",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "NvrOlTugm1c8_exLnSRPm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 898.255859375,
      "y": 2870.208984375,
      "width": 787.1394653320312,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 318948800,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 1341234752,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786684765248,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When you submit a job to Flink:\n1) The Job Manager receives the application and constructs the execution graph\n2) It allocates tasks to available slots in Task Managers\n3) Task Managers start executing their assigned tasks\n4) The Job Manager monitors execution and handles failures",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When you submit a job to Flink:\n1) The Job Manager receives the application and constructs the execution graph\n2) It allocates tasks to available slots in Task Managers\n3) Task Managers start executing their assigned tasks\n4) The Job Manager monitors execution and handles failures",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wGdgRObxP4c54qs_MeHfS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 898.900390625,
      "y": 3042.322265625,
      "width": 705.3302001953125,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3H",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1842562624,
      "version": 255,
      "versionNonce": 1424270784,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786684886841,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Even if we're not using Flink, we can borrow several lessons from its\ndesign:\n1) Separation of Time Domains: Flink's separation of processing time\nand event time is a powerful pattern that can be applied to many\ndistributed systems problems.\n2) Watermarks for Progress Tracking: The watermark concept can be\nuseful in any system that needs to track progress through unordered\nevents.\n3) State Management Patterns: Flink's approach to state management,\nincluding local state and checkpointing, can inform the design of other\nstateful distributed systems.\n4) Exactly-Once Processing: The techniques Flink uses to achieve\nexactly-once processing can be applied to other streaming systems.\n5) Resource Isolation: Flink's slot-based resource management provides\na clean way to isolate and share resources in a distributed system.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Even if we're not using Flink, we can borrow several lessons from its design:\n1) Separation of Time Domains: Flink's separation of processing time and event time is a powerful pattern that can be applied to many distributed systems problems.\n2) Watermarks for Progress Tracking: The watermark concept can be useful in any system that needs to track progress through unordered events.\n3) State Management Patterns: Flink's approach to state management, including local state and checkpointing, can inform the design of other stateful distributed systems.\n4) Exactly-Once Processing: The techniques Flink uses to achieve exactly-once processing can be applied to other streaming systems.\n5) Resource Isolation: Flink's slot-based resource management provides a clean way to isolate and share resources in a distributed system.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "qcrEjL7Es8oBqPU5p1coB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 93.255859375,
      "y": 3333.208984375,
      "width": 273.77984619140625,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1442414016,
      "version": 51,
      "versionNonce": 1500155328,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786685018474,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** Periodic check point ***",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** Periodic check point ***",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Z9dfCHImrWt-90TmH-GDA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 87.498046875,
      "y": 3370.208984375,
      "width": 672.98291015625,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 845702592,
      "version": 163,
      "versionNonce": 1487484480,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786685459519,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "By having these periodic checkpoints, we can restore the state of\nthe system from the checkpoint and resume processing from there.\nWhen a failure occurs, we stop the world and restore from the\ncheckpoint:\n1) Failure Detection: The Job Manager notices that a Task Manager\nis no longer sending heartbeats. It marks that Task Manager as\nfailed.\n2) Job Pause: The entire job is paused. All tasks, even those\nrunning on healthy Task Managers, are stopped. This is important\nbecause Flink treats the job as a whole unit for consistency.\n3) State Recovery: Flink retrieves the most recent checkpoint from\nthe state backend (which could be in memory, filesystem, or\nRocksDB depending on your configuration).\n4) Task Redistribution: The Job Manager redistributes all the tasks\nthat were running on the failed Task Manager to the remaining\nhealthy Task Managers. It may also redistribute other tasks to\nbalance the load.\n5) State Restoration: Each task restores its state from the\ncheckpoint. This means every operator gets back exactly the data\nit had processed up to the checkpoint.\n6) Source Rewind: Source operators rewind to their checkpoint\npositions. For example, a Kafka consumer would go back to the\noffset it had at checkpoint time.\n7) Resume Processing: The job resumes processing from the\ncheckpoint. Since the checkpoint contains information about exactly\nwhich records were processed, Flink guarantees exactly-once\nprocessing even after a failure.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "By having these periodic checkpoints, we can restore the state of the system from the checkpoint and resume processing from there. When a failure occurs, we stop the world and restore from the checkpoint:\n1) Failure Detection: The Job Manager notices that a Task Manager is no longer sending heartbeats. It marks that Task Manager as failed.\n2) Job Pause: The entire job is paused. All tasks, even those running on healthy Task Managers, are stopped. This is important because Flink treats the job as a whole unit for consistency.\n3) State Recovery: Flink retrieves the most recent checkpoint from the state backend (which could be in memory, filesystem, or RocksDB depending on your configuration).\n4) Task Redistribution: The Job Manager redistributes all the tasks that were running on the failed Task Manager to the remaining healthy Task Managers. It may also redistribute other tasks to balance the load.\n5) State Restoration: Each task restores its state from the checkpoint. This means every operator gets back exactly the data it had processed up to the checkpoint.\n6) Source Rewind: Source operators rewind to their checkpoint positions. For example, a Kafka consumer would go back to the offset it had at checkpoint time.\n7) Resume Processing: The job resumes processing from the checkpoint. Since the checkpoint contains information about exactly which records were processed, Flink guarantees exactly-once processing even after a failure.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
